WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.840
Pretty much everyone knows who Sauron is. For&nbsp;
a while, however, this iconic villain took on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:11.840
another persona: the Necromancer of Dol Guldur.&nbsp;
But why did this happen, and how did his rule&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:17.200
over the Hill of Sorcery come to an end? This&nbsp;
is Sauron's guise as the Necromancer explained.

00:00:18.713 --> 00:00:21.520
Sauron may be best known for&nbsp;
his role in the War of the Ring,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:26.160
but his history stretches back to the beginning&nbsp;
of time itself — and it's important to know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:30.240
his full history if you want to fully&nbsp;
understand his role as the Necromancer.

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:34.560
In the beginning, Sauron is an angelic&nbsp;
being named Mairon the Admirable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:39.600
After the world is created, Mairon becomes&nbsp;
one of the followers of the Dark Lord Morgoth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:43.520
Known at the time as Gorthaur, Sauron&nbsp;
serves Morgoth throughout the First Age,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:47.040
which ends with his master's fall&nbsp;
during the cataclysmic War of Wrath.

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:51.760
During the Second Age, Sauron picks up&nbsp;
the mantle of "Dark Lord" for himself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:56.240
and sets in motion his plan to dominate&nbsp;
Middle-Earth. Rather than waging open war,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.240 --> 00:01:00.960
however, he first tries to beguile the Elves&nbsp;
— by putting on a handsome physical form and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:05.680
befriending them under the name Annatar,&nbsp;
the Lord of Gifts. During this time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:10.960
Sauron helps the Elves forge most of the Rings&nbsp;
of Power. But disguise falls away when the Dark&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:15.680
Lord forges the One Ring in secret, hoping to&nbsp;
control the elves who wear the Rings of Power.

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:21.440
When Sauron creates the One Ring, the Elves become&nbsp;
aware of his betrayal and hide the Rings away,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:25.200
including the powerful Three Rings,&nbsp;
which Sauron had no hand in creating.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:29.040
Furious that his carefully laid&nbsp;
plans have amounted to nothing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:33.920
Sauron gathers his servants and declares open&nbsp;
war on all of the free peoples of Middle-earth.

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:40.640
Over the next 1,500 years, Sauron conquers most&nbsp;
of Middle-Earth and reclaims most of the Rings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:45.600
of Power, choosing to then redistribute them to&nbsp;
the races he believes will be easier to control:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:50.080
namely, men and dwarves. Although the&nbsp;
dwarves prove surprisingly resistant to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:55.280
the power of the Rings, the men do not —&nbsp;
and soon after, Sauron's Nazgul are born.

00:01:56.080 --> 00:02:00.640
During this era, Middle-Earth's noblest&nbsp;
and most powerful men are the Númenóreans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:05.840
who hail from the island nation of Númenór.&nbsp;
Eventually, a vast Númenórean army sails&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:09.360
east to aid the elves and put an end&nbsp;
to Sauron's rule over Middle-Earth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:13.680
Faced with inevitable defeat,&nbsp;
Sauron concocts a new plan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:18.480
and surrenders willingly to the Númenóreans, who&nbsp;
take him back to their homeland as a prisoner.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:23.440
During his time on Númenór, however, Sauron&nbsp;
corrupts the king and persuades him to invade the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:29.520
Undying Lands. This goes badly — to say the least&nbsp;
— and Númenór itself is sunk beneath the sea.

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:35.840
When Númenór sinks, Sauron goes down with it,&nbsp;
losing his physical form for the first time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:39.920
But his spirit doesn't die.&nbsp;
Instead, it returns to the mainland&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:44.480
and takes a new shape. The Silmarillion&nbsp;
explains that, once back in Mordor:

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:48.320
"[Sauron] brooded in the dark, until&nbsp;
he had wrought for himself a new shape;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.320 --> 00:02:51.360
and it was terrible, for his&nbsp;
fair semblance had departed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:54.720
for ever when he was cast into the&nbsp;
abyss at the drowning of Númenor."

00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:59.840
Unfortunately for Sauron, it turns out that&nbsp;
he wasn't the only survivor of the Númenórean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:05.120
catastrophe. A group of Númenóreans known as&nbsp;
"The Faithful" managed to escape in their ships,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:09.760
having decided not to join the invasion&nbsp;
of Valinor. Arriving in Middle-earth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:14.640
the Faithful set up two sister kingdoms:&nbsp;
Arnor in the north and Gondor in the South.

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:19.840
Seeking justice against Sauron, the&nbsp;
exiled Númenóreans join forces with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:25.040
Elves and Dwarves of Middle-Earth, sparking the&nbsp;
conflict known as the War of the Last Alliance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:30.320
The free armies win a number of successes against&nbsp;
Sauron, and eventually lay siege to Mordor itself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:34.640
During the final battle,&nbsp;
Isildur, Prince of the Faithful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:39.280
cuts the One Ring from Sauron's hand, and&nbsp;
with this, the Second Age of Middle Earth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:43.520
comes to an end — and the Dark Lord's&nbsp;
shapeless spirit takes flight once more.

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:48.160
Sauron is absent from the history&nbsp;
books of the early Third Age.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:52.560
The appendix to The Return of the King&nbsp;
succinctly explains that during this time:

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:55.120
"Sauron slept and the One Ring was lost."

00:03:55.760 --> 00:03:58.720
When he next appears, Sauron is known only as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:02.960
Necromancer — a name that has a great deal&nbsp;
of significance to Tolkien's writings.

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:09.760
By this point, Sauron has taken on a number of&nbsp;
shapes, forms, and names. The Necromancer persona,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:14.000
however, is distantly connected to the&nbsp;
earliest version of Tolkien's archvillain.

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:18.640
In the earliest versions of the stories&nbsp;
that would later appear in The Silmarillion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:24.880
one of Morgoth's chief lieutenants is Tevildo,&nbsp;
the so-called "Prince of Cats." Over time, as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:29.920
Tolkien characteristically rewrote his narrative&nbsp;
many times, the character was eventually replaced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:34.960
by a being named "Thû," who is described&nbsp;
as a magician, a sorcerer, and a werewolf.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:39.600
He's also the earliest clearly identifiable&nbsp;
version of Sauron in Tolkien's mythos.

00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:45.840
Interestingly, it's this early, underdeveloped,&nbsp;
and eventually abandoned iteration of Sauron&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:50.480
that is the most closely aligned with his&nbsp;
later appearance as the Necromancer. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:56.640
Tolkien's posthumously published book The Lays of&nbsp;
Beleriand even refers to Thû this way, explaining:

00:04:56.640 --> 00:05:01.040
"In glamoury that necromancer held his&nbsp;
hosts of phantoms and of wandering ghosts."

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:05.920
So why does the power-hungry Dark Lord&nbsp;
ultimately become associated with the title&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.920 --> 00:05:11.600
of "the Necromancer" at this point in his story?&nbsp;
The answer seems to lie in Tolkien's simple desire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:17.280
to connect his first wildly successful book, The&nbsp;
Hobbit, to his larger, then-private mythology.

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:23.200
According to Tolkien himself, the Necromancer&nbsp;
was a necessary peril who added depth to Bilbo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:27.200
Baggins' world. In a letter to his&nbsp;
publisher, Tolkien explained that:

00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:29.680
"The presence [...] of the terrible is, I believe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:35.360
what gives this imagined world its verisimilitude.&nbsp;
A safe fairy-land is untrue to all worlds."

00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:40.640
Initially, the Necromancer was meant only&nbsp;
as a veiled reference to Sauron himself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:45.520
who was unknown to the public at the time —&nbsp;
but his identity revealed itself as The Hobbit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.520 --> 00:05:50.880
nudged a little closer to the world of Tolkien's&nbsp;
other works. In another letter, Tolkien writes:

00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:54.880
"Mr Baggins began as a comic tale&nbsp;
among conventional and inconsistent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.880 --> 00:05:58.160
Grimm's fairy-tale dwarves, and&nbsp;
got drawn into the edge of [the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.160 --> 00:06:02.000
Legendarium] — so that even Sauron&nbsp;
the terrible peeped over the edge."

00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:06.880
But The Hobbit was never supposed to have been&nbsp;
consistently integrated with the stories of The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.880 --> 00:06:11.520
Silmarillion, meaning Tolkien appears to have&nbsp;
thrown the character in as a half-thought-out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:16.880
tip of the hat for his own personal amusement.&nbsp;
Then he wrote The Lord of the Rings, however, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.880 --> 00:06:22.160
everything changed. With Sauron and the One Ring&nbsp;
taking center stage for the sequel, it suddenly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.160 --> 00:06:27.520
became important to reconcile the Necromancer of&nbsp;
Mirkwood with Morgoth's second-in-command, Sauron.

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:30.720
In the appendix to The Return of the King,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:36.800
Sauron's re-emergence is first mentioned roughly&nbsp;
1,050 years into the Third Age, at which time:

00:06:36.800 --> 00:06:40.320
"A shadow falls on Greenwood, and&nbsp;
men begin to call it Mirkwood."

00:06:41.360 --> 00:06:47.840
"This is not the Greenwood of old. The very&nbsp;
air of the forest is heavy with illusion."

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:52.640
Over the next 2,000 years,&nbsp;
Sauron slowly builds his power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.640 --> 00:06:57.280
and attempts to take a new form. Tolkien's&nbsp;
details are very scarce during this time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.280 --> 00:07:01.120
and there's some contention regarding whether&nbsp;
he succeeds in taking on a physical shape&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.120 --> 00:07:06.320
at this point. While it's possible that the&nbsp;
Necromancer is spiritual in nature, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.320 --> 00:07:10.720
it's worth pointing out that Sauron is mentioned&nbsp;
as having a physical body in the novel version of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:16.480
The Lord of the Rings, so he seems to have taken&nbsp;
shape eventually. Still, physical form or no,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.480 --> 00:07:20.640
the Necromancer has little trouble sowing&nbsp;
chaos in Middle-Earth from behind the scenes.

00:07:21.680 --> 00:07:26.480
Fifty years after Sauron's shadow is first felt&nbsp;
in Mirkwood, the wizards arrive in Middle-earth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.480 --> 00:07:31.680
with the express purpose of resisting the&nbsp;
resurgent darkness. Over the next few centuries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.680 --> 00:07:36.320
several events begin to unfold, setting&nbsp;
into motion Sauron's final return.

00:07:36.320 --> 00:07:42.720
In southern Mirkwood, the elvish city of Amon Lanc&nbsp;
becomes Dol Guldur, the Hill of Sorcery — and it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:47.840
assumed by many that a Ringwraith, or some other&nbsp;
nameless evil, has taken up residence there.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.840 --> 00:07:52.400
Meanwhile, Easterlings begin to push&nbsp;
westward, displacing local settlers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.400 --> 00:07:57.360
These corrupted Men used to serve Sauron, and&nbsp;
their arrival gives their old master some local&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.360 --> 00:08:02.720
muscle to man his new fortress. This also seems&nbsp;
to be what causes the hobbits, who live just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.720 --> 00:08:07.360
west of Mirkwood, to migrate further west over&nbsp;
the Misty Mountains and settle in the Shire.

00:08:08.320 --> 00:08:12.800
By the 1,300th year of the age, the&nbsp;
Nazgûl have officially returned, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.800 --> 00:08:19.600
evil is beginning to stir… pretty much everywhere.&nbsp;
Then, roughly 1,600 years into the Third Age,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.600 --> 00:08:24.320
a sickness called the Great Plague devastates&nbsp;
Middle-earth. In The Return of the King,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.320 --> 00:08:29.600
Tolkien writes that the plague "came with dark&nbsp;
winds out of the East," and also points out that:

00:08:29.600 --> 00:08:34.000
"Later it was noted that these things happened&nbsp;
even as the Shadow grew deep in [Mirkwood],&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:38.240
and many evil things reappeared,&nbsp;
signs of the arising of Sauron."

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:43.040
About a thousand years after&nbsp;
Sauron relocates to Dol Guldur,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.040 --> 00:08:47.680
several of his enemies begin to suspect that&nbsp;
the Dark Lord himself is hiding in Mirkwood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.680 --> 00:08:53.200
rather than a lone Ringwraith. As a result,&nbsp;
Gandalf the Grey decides to go and investigate.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.760 --> 00:08:59.040
The Necromancer, however, isn't ready to reveal&nbsp;
himself. When Gandalf arrives in Dol Guldur,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.040 --> 00:09:04.000
Sauron opts to flee for the time being, so that&nbsp;
the Grey Wizard doesn't discover who he really is.

00:09:04.960 --> 00:09:08.560
The Dark Lord withdraws far into&nbsp;
the eastern regions of Middle-earth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.200 --> 00:09:13.440
In the meantime, the western portion of&nbsp;
the continent enjoys a 400-year stretch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.440 --> 00:09:17.600
of peace and quiet known as the&nbsp;
Watchful Peace. During this time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.600 --> 00:09:22.800
Sauron continues to prepare from afar, and his&nbsp;
servants in Middle-Earth remain relatively quiet.

00:09:23.840 --> 00:09:29.200
At the end of the Watchful Peace, Sauron returns&nbsp;
to Dol Guldur — with greater power and more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.200 --> 00:09:34.800
servants in tow. Coincidentally, just three short&nbsp;
years later, Gollum happens to stumble on the One&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.800 --> 00:09:39.440
Ring, and the White Council is officially formed&nbsp;
to fight the growing shadow in Middle-Earth.

00:09:40.480 --> 00:09:44.960
For almost 500 years, both the White&nbsp;
Council and the Necromancer bide their time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:51.120
Eventually, Gandalf returns to Dol Guldur in&nbsp;
search of answers. This time, he discovers that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.120 --> 00:09:56.160
the Necromancer really is Sauron, that the Dark&nbsp;
Lord is gathering together the Rings of Power,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.160 --> 00:10:00.320
and that he's desperately searching&nbsp;
for the One Ring. Gandalf also learns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.320 --> 00:10:03.840
that the enemy is trying to locate&nbsp;
and hunt down the heirs of Isildur.

00:10:05.200 --> 00:10:11.600
"Doubt ever gnaws at him. A rumour has&nbsp;
reached him. The heir of Numenor still lives."

00:10:12.560 --> 00:10:16.080
Gandalf also runs into Thorin's&nbsp;
father, Thráin , at this point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.080 --> 00:10:19.920
and the captured Dwarven king gives him&nbsp;
a map of Erebor — as well as the key&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.920 --> 00:10:23.280
that Bilbo later uses to unlock the&nbsp;
secret door to the Lonely Mountain.

00:10:24.240 --> 00:10:28.800
Equipped with this new knowledge, Gandalf urges&nbsp;
the White Council to attack the Necromancer's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.800 --> 00:10:34.080
stronghold. Saruman the White, however, delays&nbsp;
the assault, insisting that the loss of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.080 --> 00:10:39.440
One Ring means that Sauron isn't a real threat&nbsp;
to Middle-Earth. Saruman isn't actively aiding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.440 --> 00:10:44.400
Sauron at this point, but the White Wizard wants&nbsp;
to observe the Dark Lord's movements in the hopes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.400 --> 00:10:49.600
that he'll find out where Sauron thinks the One&nbsp;
Ring is — so he can find and claim it for himself.

00:10:50.800 --> 00:10:56.400
Saruman's scheming sets back the White Council's&nbsp;
attack by several decades, until, in the same year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.400 --> 00:11:01.440
that the events of The Hobbit take place, they&nbsp;
finally attack the Necromancer in Dol Guldur.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.000 --> 00:11:06.880
The fighting isn't described in any of Tolkien's&nbsp;
works, and it's unsure whether the Council took&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.880 --> 00:11:12.400
on Sauron alone, or whether the attack was&nbsp;
more of a full-scale battle. Details aside,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.400 --> 00:11:17.520
the Necromancer appears to be defeated and&nbsp;
retreats, only to later reappear in Mordor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.400 --> 00:11:23.840
Safely ensconced in his ancient home, he finally&nbsp;
openly declares himself as none other than the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.840 --> 00:11:29.440
Dark Lord Sauron himself. Soon after, the&nbsp;
War of the Ring begins, Frodo Baggins comes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.440 --> 00:11:34.400
into possession of the One Ring, and Sauron&nbsp;
the Deceiver is defeated— once and for all.

